 "standard_code": "Grade 9: 1.3 Exponents",
 "standard_url": "https://www.education.gov.za/Portals/0/CD/National%20Curriculum%20Statements%20and%20Vocational/CAPS%20SP%20%20MATHEMATICS%20GR%207-9.pdf",
 "standard_text": "Extend the general laws of exponents to include: a^m × a^n = a^(m+n), a^m ÷ a^n = a^(m−n), (a^m)^n = a^(m×n), (a × t)^n = a^n × t^n, a^0 = 1, and a^(−n) = 1/a^n; recognise and use the appropriate laws of operations using numbers involving exponents and square and cube roots.",
 "meta_desc": "Free CAPS Grade 9 exponents worksheets: laws of exponents, negative and zero exponents and exponential equations. 100 questions with memos.",
 "key_big": "a⁻ⁿ = 1 over aⁿ.",
 "key_sub": "Raise the number and the letter.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ.</li><li>a⁰ = 1.</li><li>A negative exponent means 1 over: a⁻ⁿ = <span class='fr'><span class='fn'>1</span><span class='fd'>aⁿ</span></span>. 2⁻³ = <span class='fr'><span class='fn'>1</span><span class='fd'>8</span></span>.</li><li>(3x²)³ = 27x⁶: raise the number and the letter.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>2x⁻² × 5x⁵</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2 × 5 = 10</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>x⁻²⁺⁵ = x³</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 10x³</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Negative means negative</td><td>2⁻³ = 1/8, not −8.</td></tr><tr><td>Multiplying the bases</td><td>2³ × 2⁴ = 2⁷.</td></tr><tr><td>The number in front</td><td>(3x²)³ = 27x⁶.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does CAPS Grade 9 say about exponents?",
   "a": "Use the laws of exponents, including the negative exponent a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ, to simplify expressions and solve equations."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is 5⁻¹?",
   "a": "1/5."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is (−2)⁴?",
   "a": "16."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I solve 2ˣ = 32?",
   "a": "32 = 2⁵, so x = 5."
  }
